.\" Process this file with
.\" groff -man -Tascii waitpid.2
.\"
.TH WAITPID 2 "2026-02-28" "Substrate Kernel" "System Calls Manual"
.SH NAME
waitpid, wait \- wait for process to change state
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library (libc, -lc)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sys/types.h>
.br
.B #include <sys/wait.h>
.PP
.BI "pid_t wait(int *" wstatus ");"
.br
.BI "pid_t waitpid(pid_t " pid ", int *" wstatus ", int " options ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
All of these system calls are used to wait for state changes in a child
of the calling process, and obtain information about the child whose
state has changed. A state change is considered to be: the child
terminated; the child was stopped by a signal; or the child was resumed
by a signal.
.SH RETURN VALUE
on success, returns the process ID of the child whose state has changed;
if WNOHANG was specified and one or more child(ren) specified by pid
exist, but have not yet changed state, then 0 is returned.
On error, -1 is returned, and
.I errno
is set to indicate the error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B ECHILD
No child of the caller matches
.IR pid .
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I status
or the
.I rusage
buffer points outside the caller's address space.
.TP
.B EINTR
.B WNOHANG
was not given and a signal arrived while waiting.
.TP
.B ESRCH
.I pid
is
.BR INT_MIN ,
which names no process group (as on Linux).
.SH EXAMPLES
.B Program source
.nf

#include <sys/wait.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void) {
    pid_t pid = fork();
    if (pid == 0) {
        printf("Child exiting...\\n");
        exit(1);
    } else {
        int status;
        waitpid(pid, &status, 0);
        printf("Child %d exited with status %d\\n", pid, status);
    }
    return 0;
}
.fi

.SH SEE ALSO
